WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:10.604
Software engineering is the work of making software and of keeping it in service by an engineering method.

2
00:00:10.604 --> 00:00:21.165
The formal definition, from the international vocabulary standard, is the application of a systematic, disciplined, quantifiable approach to the development, operation, and maintenance of software.

3
00:00:21.165 --> 00:00:33.839
This episode is about the map of that work — because a claim that AI ‘does software engineering’ can only be graded once you can name the area it practiced.

4
00:00:34.439 --> 00:00:41.165
The Guide to the Software Engineering Body of Knowledge — SWEBOK

5
00:00:41.165 --> 00:00:54.617
— version 4.0 was released by the IEEE Computer Society on the 15th of October 2024; version 4.0a followed in August 2026.

6
00:00:54.617 --> 00:00:59.509
It organizes the field into eighteen knowledge areas.

7
00:00:59.509 --> 00:01:11.127
Ten describe the product and its care: requirements, architecture, design, construction, testing, engineering operations, maintenance, configuration management, quality, security.

8
00:01:11.127 --> 00:01:23.968
Five describe how the work is conducted: management, process, models and methods, professional practice, economics. Three are foundations: computing, mathematics, engineering.

9
00:01:23.968 --> 00:01:32.529
Three areas are new as areas in version 4 — architecture, security, and operations.

10
00:01:33.129 --> 00:01:43.125
Version 4 gives architecture its own area, beside design. Architecture deals with stakeholders, views, styles, and decisions that are expensive to reverse.

11
00:01:43.125 --> 00:01:44.943
Design continues from there.

12
00:01:44.943 --> 00:01:55.394
In the delivery estate, the architectural decision is that delivery is its own service, owns its rows, and collaborates through a published language.

13
00:01:55.394 --> 00:02:01.301
The design decision is the Delivery aggregate, the pickup rule, and the adapters.

14
00:02:01.301 --> 00:02:07.662
A drawing of classes that never names the service boundary has recorded a design.

15
00:02:07.662 --> 00:02:15.841
The boundary is the architecture — and it is the decision the pattern chapters have been writing down.

16
00:02:16.441 --> 00:02:26.962
Maintenance is the work after delivery, and the standard ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 sorts a request into categories. Corrective repairs a discovered problem.

17
00:02:26.962 --> 00:02:35.478
Preventive corrects a latent fault before it fires. Adaptive keeps the product usable in a changed environment.

18
00:02:35.478 --> 00:02:43.494
Additive adds a function — a cancellation fee is a new rule and a new requirement.

19
00:02:43.494 --> 00:02:53.514
Perfective improves attributes with the same observable behavior. Emergency is an unscheduled temporary repair, with a corrective change still owed.

20
00:02:53.514 --> 00:03:05.037
The distinction matters once a tool can open a change: a repair, a feature, and a cleanup have different requirements and different tests.

21
00:03:05.637 --> 00:03:10.917
ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207, the 2017 edition, is the process standard.

22
00:03:10.917 --> 00:03:19.132
It groups thirty processes into four groups — agreement, organizational, technical management, and technical

23
00:03:19.132 --> 00:03:33.801
— and it is plain about what it does not do: it prescribes neither a life-cycle model, nor a development methodology, nor a modeling technique.

24
00:03:33.801 --> 00:03:35.561
The project chooses.

25
00:03:35.561 --> 00:03:50.229
The fourteen technical processes run from business analysis to disposal, and the chapter walks ‘cancel a delivery’ through all of them: the mission, the stakeholders,

26
00:03:50.229 --> 00:04:01.378
the requirements, the architecture, the design, the analysis of the tradeoff, implementation, integration, verification, transition, validation, operation, maintenance, disposal.

27
00:04:01.378 --> 00:04:06.072
Several can be open in the same week.

28
00:04:06.672 --> 00:04:14.684
Two checks, two different questions. Verification asks whether the service meets the requirement that was specified.

29
00:04:14.684 --> 00:04:20.193
Validation asks whether that requirement, once met, serves the intended use.

30
00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:33.213
A suite can pass every cancellation test and still fail validation, if customers needed to cancel after pickup and the mission analysis set that case aside.

31
00:04:33.213 --> 00:04:41.726
The given-when-then example and the quality scenario are verification. A stakeholder accepting the running product is validation.

32
00:04:42.326 --> 00:04:46.992
The life-cycle model is a decision inside the process area.

33
00:04:46.992 --> 00:04:56.326
Winston Royce drew the sequential pass in 1970 and argued in the same paper that a single pass is risky.

34
00:04:56.326 --> 00:05:03.792
Barry Boehm’s spiral, 1986, drives each cycle by the risk it reduces. The Agile Manifesto, 2001.

35
00:05:03.792 --> 00:05:14.059
Humble and Farley’s ‘Continuous Delivery’, 2010, made the path from commit to production an engineering object, and DevOps carried it into operations.

36
00:05:14.059 --> 00:05:23.858
A suite of services applies the same processes recursively, once per service, with integration and much of operations at the estate.

37
00:05:23.858 --> 00:05:34.592
And it assumes a point on the continuum: one service can be verified, released, and operated without a lockstep release of its neighbors.

38
00:05:34.592 --> 00:05:43.925
Twelve services drawn on a diagram, released only as a single batch, are a suite with a monolith’s life cycle.

39
00:05:44.525 --> 00:05:53.011
A tool that emits a controller has practiced construction. A tool that opens the merge request has touched configuration management.

40
00:05:53.011 --> 00:06:02.770
A tool that splits a service has practiced architecture — when the decision is evaluated and recorded; generation alone is not the evaluation.

41
00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:13.802
A tool that edits three services and merges them has not validated the result until a stakeholder, or a test that stands for one, accepts it.

42
00:06:13.802 --> 00:06:24.410
The human checkpoint of the AI-pattern chapter is a process control: it keeps validation and authorization in the process when a model proposes the change.

43
00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:29.501
The service boundary remains an architectural decision, held by the owning service.

44
00:06:30.101 --> 00:06:39.292
Three things to carry away. One. Eighteen knowledge areas; thirty processes; no life-cycle model prescribed. Two.

45
00:06:39.292 --> 00:06:44.462
Verification checks the requirement; validation checks the use. Three.

46
00:06:44.462 --> 00:06:53.653
A tool’s work is graded by the area it practices and by what it still owes.

47
00:06:54.253 --> 00:07:02.126
Next, the AI system itself: thirteen ways to arrange a model call — and the one rule that never moves.
